From: i7m7r8 <i7m7r8@users.noreply.github.com>
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: dts: qcom: sm4450: name the bus node soc, not soc@0

of_device_make_bus_id() (drivers/of/device.c) names a platform device from the
node's first reg value, and only when the node has no reg does it fall back to
kbasename(full_name), which keeps the unit address.  The SM4450 bus node has
ranges and #address-cells but no reg of its own, so upstream's "soc@0" makes
the device - and every sysfs path below it - read

	/sys/devices/platform/soc@0/1d84000.ufshc

Android cannot be retargeted away from that.  First stage builds
/dev/block/by-name/* itself: DeviceHandler takes each partition's sysfs path,
strips "/devices/platform/" and keeps only what matches an entry of
androidboot.boot_devices, so the two spellings have to agree character for
character.  Verified in QEMU with the ROM's own /init and a GPT disk carrying
the PARTNAMEs this fstab asks for (harness in this repo's docs, kernel and
initrd from the CI artifact, only the DT and the bootargs changing):

  soc     + boot_devices=soc/<dev>       mounts /metadata and /system
  soc     + boot_devices=<dev>           partition(s) not found after polling
                                          timeout: system, vendor, metadata
  soc@0   + boot_devices=soc/<dev>        same failure, and it is the one the
                                          phone sees: "Failed to mount required
                                          partitions early ..."
  soc@0   + boot_devices=soc@0/<dev>      mounts

So the bootargs alone could be bent to fit soc@0, and that is what
dts/sm4450-xiaomi-flame.dts now says; but second stage and the vendor HALs
have their own copy of the path and cannot be bent at all.  The ROM's
vendor_ueventd.rc, init.target.rc, vendor.qti.smp2p.rc and
init.qcom.early_boot.sh all spell /sys/devices/platform/soc/..., as does
fstab.default's sysfs_path= on the /data line.  Renaming the node once makes
every one of those paths real; leaving it named soc@0 would leave the port
fixed only as far as the cmdline can reach.

Upstream keeps the unit address because it is harmless to a Linux userspace
that reads the DT for paths; it is the Android vendor userspace that hardcodes
them.  Drop the address, keep the label, so &soc references in the board DTS
are untouched.

---
--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/sm4450.dtsi
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/sm4450.dtsi
@@ -393,7 +393,7 @@
 		};
 	};
 
-	soc: soc@0 {
+	soc: soc {
 		#address-cells = <2>;
 		#size-cells = <2>;
 		ranges = <0 0 0 0 0x10 0>;
